#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Architecture"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "This page draws the architecture at three levels, using the C4 model. C1 shows the system in its context. C2 shows the containers inside it. C3 goes into the components of the containers where most of the design lives."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Every box is a capability, or a group of capabilities, from the [catalogue]{1}. You won't see any product names, because choosing products is up to each implementation ([implementations]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The colours follow C4 conventions. People are dark blue, the system and its containers are blue, and external systems are grey."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "C1 System context"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Why it is drawn this way"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Changes reach the platform only through CI. Engineers and leads work on the CI host, never on the platform directly ([P1]{1}). So when you see an arrow from people to the platform, it means they use it, not that they change it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "There can be more than one CI host. A repository can live on several hosts, because the architecture depends on federation, which every mainstream host offers, rather than on any one of them ([CAP-REPOSITORIES.5]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Recipients sit outside the boundary, because data only leaves as a de-identified publication ([P15]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The identity provider belongs to the organisation, not to the platform ([CAP-IDENTITY.1]{1}). Learners, guardians and the public sign in separately, and they never join platform groups ([CAP-IDENTITY.6]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Exchanges with other government systems run in both directions, and each one is declared and reconciled ([CAP-INTEROPERABILITY]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "C2 Containers: trust and delivery"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Each row here is a layer, and each layer is a repository. An arrow from a repository to an identity is a CI run. The run exchanges the token the CI host gives it for that identity, which is what federation means. Notice that no arrow starts on a workstation."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The rows are levels of trust, and each row is a repository ([P2]{1}). One row's identity can't act in another row, because its federated credential trusts only one repository."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Only the bootstrap row is started by people. It declares who CI is, so a merge on its own must never change it ([P4]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The platform reaches products only through what it generates from the tenant registry, meaning grants, schemas and published outputs. The product never reads the platform's state ([CAP-ONBOARDING.3]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The lines for modules and for trust are dashed because neither is a call at run time. Modules are source code, and credentials are declarations."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "C2 Containers: the data platform"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The catalog sits in the middle because every read and write of analytical data goes through it. That's why one set of policies is enough ([CAP-CATALOG]{1}, [CAP-COMPUTE.3]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Landing feeds the pipeline, and the pipeline feeds the archive and quarantine, because landing is a queue rather than a place to keep things ([CAP-INGESTION.2]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The product app talks only to its own operational database. Analytics reach that database through the catalog, never over a direct connection ([CAP-OPERATIONALDB.7]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Dashboards read the semantic layer rather than tables, so each measure has one definition ([P11]{1}). The [semantic layer]{2} page shows how that one definition reaches every consumer."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Publication is a separate store, and only models write to it, so nothing reaches a recipient without going through de-identification ([CAP-SHARING.1]{1}). Open statistics leave through the portal after disclosure control ([CAP-PUBLICATION]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Registries and collection sit with the products because they're operational. They hold the authoritative records and returns that everything downstream counts ([CAP-MASTERDATA]{1}, [CAP-COLLECTION]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "C3 Components: access decision"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "This is how the catalog decides what a single query returns."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "There are three inputs, and each has a different owner ([P14]{1}). The platform team owns the groups, a loader owns the entitlements, and stewards own the classification. So no single change can widen all three ([CAP-ACCESS.6]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The decision is a chain, and each step can only narrow it. Reach lets you at the table, and then the row filter and masks can only take things away ([CAP-ACCESS.3]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The exemption list is dashed and kept short, because every entry on it is a path to raw data ([CAP-ACCESS.4]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "C3 Components: product pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "This is how the example product processes a file, from the moment it arrives to the released result. The [document-review product]{1} page describes it in full."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The stages are separate jobs joined by storage. So a stage can't get ahead of the one before it, and you can retry each one on its own ([CAP-PIPELINE.3]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Only the publish stage touches the database. The three stages before it keep working even when the database is unavailable ([CAP-PIPELINE.6]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "Review sits beside the pipeline rather than inside it. People decide at their own pace, and their decisions outrank any re-run ([CAP-REVIEW.3]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/README.md
msgid "The gate reads both the database and the register snapshot, because before anything is released it needs to know which subjects were expected, as well as the results ([CAP-RELEASE.1]{1})."
msgstr ""
